WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.300
ID tokens, access tokens, and refresh tokens have different purposes.

2
00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:08.840
An ID token tells a client application about the signed-in user.

3
00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:16.860
An access token is intended for a resource API and is the token an API should validate before authorizing API operations.

4
00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:24.560
Refresh tokens are used by clients to obtain new tokens and should be protected according to application type and hosting environment.

5
00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:31.160
A web API should make authorization decisions from validated access tokens intended for that API.

6
00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:40.700
Token validation should be performed by maintained components that check issuer, audience, lifetime, signature, signing keys, and token type expectations.

7
00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:44.160
Claims should be interpreted only after validation succeeds.

8
00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:47.000
Claims require careful interpretation.

9
00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:58.300
Scopes, roles, tenant IDs, user IDs, app IDs, groups, and optional claims may vary by tenant, flow, configuration, and token type.

10
00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:01.440
A claim that appears in one environment may be absent in another.

11
00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:06.900
Group claims can be limited or represented differently when a user has many group memberships.

12
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:11.960
Optional claims should be treated as configuration-dependent, not universal facts.

13
00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:16.160
A valid token does not automatically authorize every operation.

14
00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:20.020
It proves that the token passed validation for its intended use.

15
00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:23.080
The application still decides what the caller may do.

16
00:01:23.180 --> 00:01:35.340
Resource-level authorization may require trusted server-side checks for ownership, tenant membership, subscription state, data classification, licensing, or application policy.

17
00:01:35.620 --> 00:01:42.740
Good authorization code keeps token validation, claims interpretation, and business authorization separate enough to review.

18
00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:50.600
The system first validates the token, then interprets the claims it needs, then checks server-side policy for the specific operation and resource.
